Since 2023 I began studying Arabic and Arab culture as a hobby, and was fascinated with how different they are and surprised that the people there “put up” with all sorts of exploits against them. Ironically, I learned that we aren’t any different in the west, our politicians exploit us all the time. But the local culture heavily influences what exploits do and don’t work on the people, and every population is exploited by their leaders in one way or another, for some reason or another.

While doing my deep dive on Arab culture, I came across Afghanistan and the conditions Afghans live in. It breaks my heart that the people there have been subject to so much struggle and suffering. I also speak Russian as a second language, and upon learning that Afghanistan’s neighbours speak Russian, I saw fit to visit them to study adjacent cultures in person. I fell in love with the Tajik culture and see the similarities it has with the Afghan culture, whether either culture wants to admit it or not.

Now, for all the studying, I noticed patterns and determined for myself that Afghanistan is a ticking time bomb and too many political tensions surrounding it are getting tighter, and some time soon something will probably give. I began telling people that Afghanistan is on the verge of civil war and war with it’s neighbours, and I’ve been called crazy and a nutjob for it, but now (October 7, 2026) I see it unravelling and my theories coming to life.

I am writing this blog post for two purposes: One, to be boastful, I want to post these analysis’ before my predictions come true so that I can say, “I told you so!”, and time stamps on my posts will verify that, and two, that in case any policy makers (So civilian defense intelligence analysts) come across this blog, for them to understand what they are getting into.

Leaders have a responsibility to everyone effected by their decisions. However, it so happens that policy makers think they only have an obligation to be responsible to their nationals. This is a violation of universal ethics that everyone ought to follow because we are all sharing this world. And world leaders like to be present themselves as righteous in their policy, but when it comes to global affairs, most decisions have more supressed consequences than anything.

Don’t get me wrong, policy makers have the most difficult job of putting the interest of their people first, and the human condition means that people will always fight for more than they need, and in a global economy like what we’ve got today, that forces nations to be ruthless against each other and in international policy. If leaders aren’t competitive, the competitive ones will have no issue walking right on them. They must therefore fight for their own on every front to make sure their people get their piece of the pie.

However, if only we understood our neighbours better, we could make policies that symbiotically strengthen both parties, and don’t cause one or the other to build up resentment, and we can all get more than enough for ourselves. And most of the western world has exactly that. But there are too many fronts between the east and west that are neglected, and I would have it that they got a piece of the pie too.
